Chapter: Fat16CycleGuard

  cites Foreword chapter Fat16
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 The fixture's FAT has been patched so cluster 2 points to 3 and 3 points
 back to 2, in both FAT copies. Every number in that cycle is a VALID cluster
 on this volume, so the address guard cannot see it: `fat16-cluster-ok`
 answers yes to both, and the walk is well-formed at every single step. What
 is malformed is the chain, and only a bound on the WALK catches it.

 Searching a directory chain for a name that is not there is what forces the
 whole walk: it cannot stop early on a hit. Without fuel this call does not
 return, so the ablation of this arm is a HANG rather than a wrong answer,
 and the harness reports it as a timeout. That is a weaker signal than a
 moved row and it is worth saying so rather than dressing it up.

Section: Reporting

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing = act
    vol <- fat16-init 2048
    print-line-uni ("clusters " & show (vol.vol-cluster-count))
    print-line-uni ("cycle-clusters-are-valid c2=" & yn (fat16-cluster-ok vol 2) & " c3=" & yn (fat16-cluster-ok vol 3))
    two <- fat16-next-cluster vol 2
    three <- fat16-next-cluster vol 3
    print-line-uni ("fat 2->" & show two & " 3->" & show three)
    hit <- fat16-find-in-cluster-dir vol 2 "NOSUCH.XXX"
    print-line-uni (when hit is Just (e) -> "found " & (e.de-name) is None -> "walk-terminated none")
  end
